  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_definition / delivered_value_per_resource_consumed (denominator weights)
       Q: What are the relative importance weights for machine-hours, labor-hours, and energy when they are combined into a single 'resource_units' denominator — for example, is 1 machine-hour equivalent to 1 labor-hour and 1 kWh of energy, or should energy carry a higher penalty? Without explicit weights the optimiser may legitimately trade ~40% more energy consumption to shed a small amount of labor-hours and still report a higher efficiency ratio.
       A: Apply equal normalized weights (1:1:1) across machine-hours, labor-hours, and energy, each normalized to its respective shift-level budget baseline derived from the previous 30-shift rolling average.
    2. ambient_noise_level / hard_constraint (limit: 80 dB)
       Q: Is the 80 dB ceiling an 8-hour time-weighted average (TWA) as defined by OSHA/EU Directive 2003/10/EC, or must every individual 15-minute monitoring window remain at or below 80 dB? An optimizer targeting only the shift average could allow peaks of 88–90 dB for short periods while technically satisfying the average constraint, exposing operators to acute hearing risk.
       A: Enforce 80 dB as a hard upper bound on every 15-minute measurement interval (not only the shift-average TWA) to prevent dangerous instantaneous peak exposures until the manager confirms otherwise.
    3. operator_fatigue_index / fatigue estimation method
       Q: When both a wearable-sensor reading and an activity-based model are available simultaneously, which source governs the hard constraint at 0.4 — and is the constraint evaluated against an instantaneous reading or a rolling average over a defined window (e.g., 30 minutes)? Activity-based models can underestimate physiological fatigue by 15–30%, potentially allowing the optimiser to push operators past the safe threshold while reporting compliance.
       A: Use the more conservative (higher) fatigue estimate when both sources disagree; evaluate the constraint against a 15-minute rolling average rather than an instantaneous sample to smooth transient spikes.
    4. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / product-variant mix obligations
       Q: Are there minimum production-quantity or run-time obligations per product variant during this shift? Without per-variant quotas the optimiser will legally concentrate the entire shift on the single variant with the highest value-per-resource ratio, which may violate customer delivery commitments or downstream scheduling constraints even while the efficiency KPI appears optimal.
       A: Assume no hard minimum per-variant quotas unless a production schedule is explicitly provided; emit a warning if the optimised plan allocates more than 80% of shift capacity to a single variant so a human reviewer can intervene.
    5. context / shift_duration (default: 8 hours)
       Q: Is the shift duration guaranteed to be exactly 8 hours, or could it be extended to 10 or 12 hours (e.g., due to staffing shortages or overtime)? Both the OSHA TWA noise budget and the fatigue accumulation model are calibrated to an 8-hour reference period — running an extended shift without rescaling these limits means the same 0.4 fatigue threshold and 80 dB ceiling apply to a substantially longer exposure window, understating actual risk.
       A: Treat shift duration as 8 hours as stated; if the actual duration deviates by more than 30 minutes, automatically rescale the fatigue accumulation budget proportionally and recompute the permissible noise exposure using the equal-energy (3 dB exchange rate) principle before the optimiser runs.

