  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. resource_efficiency (denominator aggregation)
       Q: How should labor, energy, materials, and machine_time be combined into a single 'total_resource_consumed' denominator — by monetary cost (e.g. labor at $35/hr, energy at $0.15/kWh), physical units, or a normalised index — given that different weightings can shift the optimizer's preferred production mode by 30–50% and may cause it to aggressively substitute one resource type for another?
       A: Apply monetary cost weights sourced from the site's current period standard cost sheet; if unavailable, assign equal fractional weight to each of the four resource types (0.25 each), which preserves symmetry but may over-represent cheap resources such as energy.
    2. context.value_metric (throughput_or_output_quality_adjusted)
       Q: Is 'delivered value' measured as (a) units produced × unit margin, (b) quality-adjusted yield (units × pass-rate × quality score × price), or (c) raw volume throughput — and does rework count as consumed resource without added value — because the optimizer's objective function cannot be constructed without a precise formula?
       A: Use quality-adjusted throughput: delivered_value = units_produced × first-pass-yield × standard_unit_margin, with rework units counted in resource consumption but excluded from delivered value; revert to raw throughput if quality data are unavailable.
    3. operator_fatigue (threshold interpretation: average vs. instantaneous cap)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue limit a shift-long time-weighted average or a hard ceiling that must not be exceeded at any single measurement point, given that an average of 0.4 can legally permit sustained peaks of 0.6–0.7 in the first half of the shift — a pattern that may violate ergonomic safety intent even while satisfying the constraint as written?
       A: Treat 0.4 as a rolling 30-minute moving-average ceiling (not a shift-mean), and additionally enforce that no single 5-minute reading exceeds 0.60, preventing exploitable burst patterns while aligning with the stated HumanWellbeing priority.
    4. resource_efficiency (no saturation / rate cap)
       Q: Should the optimizer be permitted to simultaneously run all machines at maximum throughput rate if doing so increases resource efficiency, even if it pushes ambient noise and operator fatigue to within 1–2% of their hard limits — or is there a desired operating headroom (e.g. stay ≤ 75 dB and ≤ 0.35 fatigue) to absorb unplanned variance during the shift?
       A: Apply a 10% safety headroom by default: treat effective noise ceiling as 72 dB and effective fatigue ceiling as 0.36, leaving buffer for sensor noise and unscheduled events; tighten only if the manager explicitly approves saturation-level operation.
    5. minimum_output_floor (absent from contract)
       Q: Is there a minimum acceptable output volume or throughput rate for this shift (e.g. at least 80% of the baseline shift plan) — because maximising value-per-resource-unit is mathematically satisfied by a very small, highly selective micro-batch that may deliver excellent efficiency ratios while leaving the production schedule severely short?
       A: Enforce a minimum output floor of 85% of the shift's planned production volume (sourced from the MES daily schedule); if no plan exists, set the floor at 90% of the trailing 30-day average shift output, so the efficiency optimisation cannot be gamed via volume reduction.

