    - Option 2 — Noise Constraint Temporary Relaxation with Mandatory PPE Protocol (Moderate): Relax the operational Noise ceiling from 80.0 dB to 85.0 dB for a capped 30-day window while mandating Class-B hearing protection (SNR ≥ 27 dB) for all floor workers, reducing effective exposure to below 58 dB at the ear. Simultaneously implement mandatory rest intervals to bring FatigueIndex to ≤ 0.40 through pacing adjustments. This allows production throughput to rise enough to meet roughly 98% of the new demand target. Medical audiometry baseline checks must be completed before relaxation takes effect. Noise constraint is temporarily relaxed; FatigueIndex constraint is preserved.
      Preserves human constraints: No
    - Option 3 — Dual Constraint Relaxation with Compensatory Health Safeguards (Last Resort): Temporarily raise both the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.62 and the Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB for a maximum 14-day window to fully absorb the 25% demand spike. Mandatory compensatory measures must accompany this option: (a) mandatory 24-hour recovery days after any shift where FatigueIndex exceeds 0.50, (b) full PPE compliance for Noise, (c) daily supervisor fatigue check-ins, and (d) automatic revert to original limits after 14 days regardless of demand status. This option fully meets the demand deadline but carries the highest worker-health risk and requires written manager sign-off and HR notification before activation.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
