  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order vs context._raw_priority_order
       Q: Which priority ordering governs trade-off resolution: the single-pillar 'HumanWellbeing' (which subordinates efficiency entirely) or the raw KPI order [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, noise_level] (which puts efficiency first)? These two fields contradict each other and will produce opposite decisions whenever a small fatigue gain can be traded for a large efficiency gain.
       A: Apply 'HumanWellbeing' as the lexicographic top priority, meaning no efficiency gain is accepted if it causes operator_fatigue or noise_level to breach their hard limits; resource_efficiency is maximised only within the feasible region defined by those constraints.
    2. resource_efficiency (maximize, threshold: null)
       Q: Is there an upper cap on any individual resource category — for example, a maximum energy draw per shift (e.g. ≤ 120 % of baseline kWh) or a maximum overtime-labour multiplier — so the optimiser cannot exploit a cheap resource without limit to inflate the efficiency ratio?
       A: Cap each resource type at 110 % of its historical shift baseline; if baseline data are unavailable, treat the current operating level as the ceiling and allow no more than a 10 % increase in any single resource category.
    3. resource_efficiency — definition of 'delivered value'
       Q: What constitutes 'delivered value' in the numerator: units shipped, revenue-weighted output, quality-adjusted throughput (e.g. excluding rework), or something else — and does partially completed work-in-progress at shift end count?
       A: Use quality-adjusted units completed and handed off before shift end as the value numerator; work-in-progress not yet at a handoff gate is excluded, and reworked units count only once at their post-rework grade.
    4. assumptions.resource_types_included (labor in denominator)
       Q: If labour-hours appear in the resource denominator, the optimiser can raise efficiency by reducing headcount or deferring breaks — both of which increase individual operator fatigue. Should labour be excluded from the denominator, or is there a minimum-staffing hard constraint (e.g. ≥ N operators on the floor at all times)?
       A: Include labour-hours in the denominator but add a hard constraint that the number of active operators cannot fall below the shift's planned staffing level, and that scheduled break intervals are inviolable.
    5. noise_level (threshold: 80.0 dB, measurement point: operator_workstation)
       Q: Is the 80 dB limit a peak instantaneous reading or an 8-hour time-weighted average (TWA) per ISO 9612, and — if there are multiple workstations — must every station stay below 80 dB, or only the station currently occupied by each operator?
       A: Treat 80 dB as an 8-hour TWA per ISO 9612; apply the constraint to every manned workstation simultaneously so that rotating operators between stations cannot be used to average away localised noise violations.

