    1. operator_fatigue / fatigue_measurement
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue hard limit enforced as an instantaneous ceiling throughout the entire shift (never exceed 0.4 at any moment), or as an end-of-shift aggregate/average — because an end-of-shift reading of 0.4 is compatible with mid-shift peaks of 0.7+ that expose operators to real ergonomic harm?
       A: Treat 0.4 as a rolling instantaneous ceiling: the constraint is violated the moment the fatigue index exceeds 0.4 at any sampled point during the shift, not only at end-of-shift.
    2. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order conflict
       Q: The normalised priority_order field records only 'HumanWellbeing' as the governing priority, while _raw_priority_order lists resource_efficiency first — which ordering should the optimiser use when an efficiency gain would push fatigue or noise from, say, 0.30 to 0.38 (still within limits): is human wellbeing strictly dominant so constraint slack is always preserved, or may the optimiser trade slack for efficiency as long as hard limits are not breached?
       A: HumanWellbeing is strictly dominant: the optimiser preserves a 10 % safety margin below both hard limits (fatigue ≤ 0.36, noise ≤ 72 dB(A)) before maximising resource_efficiency, rather than driving to the constraint boundary.
    3. resource_efficiency (no satiation threshold)
       Q: resource_efficiency is set to maximize with no upper satiation point, so the optimiser will push efficiency to the absolute limit permitted by fatigue and noise constraints — is there a 'good enough' efficiency target (e.g., ≥ 15 % above baseline) above which residual constraint slack should be banked rather than traded away for marginal further gains?
       A: No satiation threshold is applied; the optimiser maximises resource_efficiency subject to the hard constraints. If a safety-margin default is accepted in the priority_order ambiguity above, that margin implicitly caps how aggressively efficiency is pursued.
    4. value_metric definition
       Q: The assumption allows 'production units or monetary equivalent' — if the shift produces a mixed variant set where high-margin SKUs require 20–30 % more machine speed (and thus higher noise and fatigue), should the optimiser maximise revenue-weighted value (favouring premium variants) or unit-count throughput (treating all variants equally)?
       A: Use the site-standard monetary value per SKU if a price list is available; otherwise fall back to uniform unit count, and flag the ambiguity for the manager before the schedule is committed.
    5. noise_level measurement (LAeq vs instantaneous ceiling)
       Q: The assumptions specify LAeq (equivalent continuous A-weighted level), which allows brief spikes well above 80 dB(A) as long as the time-weighted average stays at 80 — is the 80 dB(A) limit intended as an LAeq over the full shift, or as a true instantaneous ceiling that must never be exceeded at any moment (closer to OSHA's impulse-noise provisions)?
       A: Interpret 80 dB(A) as an instantaneous peak ceiling at the operator workstation: any moment exceeding 80 dB(A) constitutes a constraint violation, providing a stricter and safer interpretation than a pure LAeq average.

